# IMPERIA CW — Cold War: West Germany integration from quaZa e45483ff.
"""Flavor texts: the chancellery pillar, part 2."""
D = {
"constitutional_reform": "Twenty years after Herrenchiemsee the Bundestag sets up an Enquete Commission to examine the whole Basic Law: federalism, the role of parliament, the emergency constitution, the place of the citizen. Its experts will write a thousand pages. Whether those pages gather dust in the archives or become the blueprint for a new republic depends on a government willing to use them.",
"direct_democracy": "Every Land constitution knows the referendum; the Basic Law knows it only for redrawing the borders of the Länder. The memory of Weimar - of plebiscites used to destroy the Republic - kept the people's vote out of Bonn. A generation later, citizens' initiatives against motorways and power stations ask whether the Federal Republic still needs to be afraid of its own citizens.",
"elected_president": "Since 1949 the Federal Convention has chosen the head of state, precisely so that he would have no mandate of his own. A president elected by the people would be a counterweight to the chancellor and a voice beyond the parties - exactly what the fathers of the Basic Law feared after Hindenburg. Times have changed, say the reformers. Have they?",
"laender_reform": "Bremen is smaller than many cities, Bavaria larger than many countries. The Ernst Commission proposes five or six strong Länder instead of eleven unequal ones: a Nordstaat around Hamburg, the Saar merged with Rhineland-Palatinate. Article 29 of the Basic Law even commands a reorganisation. Every single Land government is against it.",
"emergency_laws_review": "The emergency constitution of 1968 brought hundreds of thousands onto the streets and gave the extra-parliamentary opposition its name. A liberal majority could now tie it more tightly: no decrees without the Constitutional Court's review, no Joint Committee without the Bundesrat, no use of the army at home except in the gravest emergency. The Interior Ministry warns against disarming the state.",
"first_past_the_post": "The Grand Coalition promised it in 1966: British-style constituencies, one member per seat, the strongest candidate wins - and the FDP would vanish from the Bundestag. Whoever now governs with a majority of their own could finish the job and end coalition politics for good. The smaller parties call it a coup by electoral law, and they are not entirely wrong.",
"three_percent": "The five-per-cent hurdle was the Republic's answer to Weimar's splinter parties - and it keeps out new movements just as effectively. Lowering it to three per cent would make the Bundestag more colourful and more representative. Some of the new colours would be welcome; others have names that the Republic hoped never to hear in its parliament again.",
"changing_society": "Long hair in the Bundestag, communes in Kreuzberg, rock music on the radio, women who want a career rather than their husband's permission to work. The republic of Adenauer is turning into the republic of 1968, and the change runs through every family dinner table. The government can try to lead it, slow it down or simply ride it.",
"paragraph_218": "'My belly belongs to me': in June 1971, 374 women declare on the cover of Stern that they have had abortions - a crime under a paragraph of the Criminal Code written in 1871. The churches mobilise, the women's movement takes to the streets, and Karlsruhe waits for the law that the coalition will have to write. No paragraph divides the country more deeply.",
"equal_rights": "The Basic Law has promised equal rights for men and women since 1949; the labour market never quite believed it. In 1980, because Brussels insists, a German statute finally says that women and men must be paid the same for the same work and may not be discriminated against when hired. It is a beginning - and the employers' associations make sure it remains only that.",
"comprehensive_schools": "At ten, German children are sorted into Gymnasium, Realschule or Hauptschule - and with them, all too often, their whole future. The comprehensive school would keep all children together until sixteen. Hesse and North Rhine-Westphalia try it; parents' associations collect millions of signatures against it; and the school question becomes a class question all over again.",
"tripartite_schools": "The three-tier school is a German pride: the Gymnasium for the academic few, the Realschule for the clerks and technicians, the dual apprenticeship for the skilled workers who make 'Made in Germany' a promise. Bavaria and Baden-Württemberg defend it as the secret of their success. Achievement, they say, must pay off - and it starts at ten.",
"university_framework": "One federal law for all the universities of the Republic: the Universities Framework Act decides who governs the institution - the professors alone, as before 1968, or a group university in which assistants and students have a vote. The Constitutional Court has already said that professors must keep the majority on questions of research. Everything else is politics.",
"census": "In 1983 every household in the Federal Republic is to answer the state's questions: who lives here, who works where, how many rooms, how many cars. A grassroots movement calls for a boycott - 'Only sheep let themselves be counted' - and the Constitutional Court is asked to stop it. Its answer will invent a new fundamental right: the right to informational self-determination.",
"private_television": "January 1984: a cable pilot project in Ludwigshafen launches the first private television channel in the Federal Republic. The Union wants an end to what it calls 'red radio' and the dominance of the public broadcasters; the SPD fears a German Berlusconi. Satellites are already in orbit. The question is not whether private television comes, but on whose terms.",
"public_broadcasting": "ARD and ZDF remain the only channels: independent of the state, financed by licence fees, supervised by broadcasting councils in which churches, unions, employers and parties all have a seat. It is the Allies' legacy and the Republic's pride - a television that has to answer to society rather than to the market. Its critics call it a bureaucracy with a camera.",
"citizenship_reform": "Millions of children of guest workers were born in Germany, speak with a Swabian or Ruhr accent and have never lived anywhere else - and they are not German. Citizenship by place of birth, and perhaps a second passport, would change that. For the reformers it is overdue; for the Union it is the end of the nation as a community of descent.",
"flick_affair": "The tax investigators were looking for evasion. What they found was a ledger: 'cultivating the political landscape', and next to it the names of almost every party treasurer in Bonn - and of two economics ministers. The Flick group paid millions to the parties while asking the government for tax relief on its share sales. The Republic learns what its politics cost, and who paid.",
"party_finance_law": "After the scandals comes the law: donations above a threshold must be published with the donor's name, and the parties receive state money in proportion to the votes they win. The established parties had wanted to secure their finances. The unintended winners are the Greens, who become rich overnight on the strength of their voters.",
"ecological_restructuring": "'Fortschritt 90' is the SPD's attempt to reconcile the Ruhr with the forest: energy taxes that make pollution expensive, a boom in environmental technology, an industrial policy for a country that has run out of space for smokestacks. The unions fear for the steel and coal jobs; the Greens fear that the SPD will steal their programme. Both are right.",
"shorter_week": "Four million unemployed and an economy that produces more with fewer people every year. Lafontaine's answer: share the work. The 35-hour week, limits on overtime, a right to part-time employment - a programme for full employment paid for with time rather than money. The employers call it a recipe for decline; IG Metall has been demanding it for years.",
"red_green_pact": "Lafontaine offers the Greens what Brandt never offered: a coalition of equals rather than a junior partner to be tamed. The Realos are tempted by ministries and a chance to shut down the reactors. The Fundis want the withdrawal from NATO written into the coalition agreement. Whether the pact becomes a government depends on the voters - and on what each side is willing to give up.",
"wealth_tax": "A wealth tax, a higher top rate of income tax and an end to the loopholes on capital gains: the money for rebuilding the East and for the jobs programme is to come from those who have it. The unions applaud, the business associations threaten to invest abroad, and the Bundesbank warns of capital flight. Social democracy has rediscovered its old enemy.",
"bundesbank_truce": "No more public demands for lower interest rates, no more feuds with the President of the Bundesbank on the evening news, no more 'the most dangerous man in Europe' on the front page of The Sun. The finance minister makes his peace with Frankfurt. The markets calm down. The party's left wonders what it voted for.",
"european_employment_pact": "Keynes on a continental scale: coordinated wage policy, public investment programmes and pressure on the central banks for lower interest rates across the whole Community. One country alone cannot spend its way to full employment, the chancellor argues - but twelve together might. It all depends on whether Paris is willing to join in.",
"two_thirds_reforms": "Together the two big parties hold the two-thirds majority in both chambers that every amendment of the Basic Law requires. What they agree on, nobody can stop: not the small parties, not the Länder, not the street. A grand coalition is the moment for the reforms that are too big for any single camp - and a temptation to rewrite the rules in its own favour.",
"finance_reform": "Who pays for universities, hospitals and motorways - the Federation or the Länder? The grand coalition untangles the federal finances: joint tasks shared between Bonn and the state capitals, a fiscal equalisation that the rich south hates and the poor north needs. It is dry, complicated and decisive for how the Republic will function for decades.",
"concerted_action_revived": "Karl Schiller's round table returns: trade unions, employers' associations, the Bundesbank and both coalition partners meet in the economics ministry to agree on wages, prices and investment. 'Social symmetry', Schiller called it. It works as long as everyone gives something - and ends the moment someone feels cheated.",
"small_parties_rise": "With both big parties in the government, the opposition happens elsewhere: in the small parties of parliament, in the student unions, in the streets, and on the edges of the political spectrum where nobody in Bonn is looking. History has a precedent - the grand coalition of 1966 gave birth to the extra-parliamentary opposition and the NPD's best years.",
"renew_grand_coalition": "Another four years together. The country is governed calmly, the big reforms pass, the budget is balanced. But the two great parties begin to look alike, their members ask what they are still fighting for, and the voters who want an alternative look for one beyond the two of them.",
"end_grand_coalition": "Enough of the marriage of convenience. The chancellor's party wants to govern with a smaller partner again - one it can lead rather than negotiate with. Whether that is possible depends on the arithmetic of the last election. If it is not, the grand coalition may outlive the will to continue it.",
"csu_land_parties": "The 'circles of friends of the CSU' in Hesse, Lower Saxony and Baden-Württemberg become Land associations of their own. Wherever the CDU is too soft for Strauß's taste, the CSU now stands its own candidates. The conservative voter outside Bavaria finally has a choice. The CDU calls it treason.",
"cdu_in_bavaria": "If the CSU marches north, the CDU marches south. A Bavarian Land association, candidates in every constituency between Hof and Lindau, a CDU poster in every Bavarian village. For thirty years the sister parties divided Germany between them. That arrangement is over.",
"sister_war": "Two Christian parties are fighting for the same voters in the same constituencies. The SPD smiles, the FDP rejoices at every split constituency, and the joint parliamentary group of the CDU and CSU in the Bundestag - the foundation of every Union government since 1949 - is about to break apart for good.",
"strauss_of_the_right": "'There must be no democratically legitimate party to the right of the CSU.' Strauß's maxim was meant for Bavaria; now it is to apply to the whole country. The CSU absorbs the national conservatives, the disappointed expellees and the law-and-order voters before anyone further to the right can claim them. It is a strategy of containment - and a risk of contagion.",
"barzel_eastern_treaties": "Chancellor Barzel will not tear up Brandt's treaties with Moscow and Warsaw; the damage to Bonn's reputation would be too great. But he will put them into force only together with a Bundestag resolution that keeps the German question open and the claim to unity alive. The East grumbles. The treaties stand.",
"barzel_property": "Every employee a small capitalist: savings premiums, employee shares in the company, the 'investment wage' that the Union's Christian labour wing has wanted since the 1950s. Property for the many, says the chancellor, is the best answer to the socialists. The unions suspect a plan to buy off their members.",
"barzel_majority": "Barzel governs with a handful of defectors from the SPD and FDP, men whom their former parties call traitors and whom his own party does not quite trust. Every vote in the Bundestag could be the last. The chancellor spends his evenings on the telephone and his nights counting heads.",
"barzel_europe": "Barzel's alternative to Brandt's Ostpolitik is a political union of Western Europe: a Community with a common foreign policy, a European parliament with real powers and a framework in which the German question can one day be answered without frightening Germany's neighbours. Paris listens with interest. London, not yet a member, listens with suspicion.",
"first_green_minister": "December 1985: Joschka Fischer takes the oath as Hesse's environment minister in white trainers and a tweed jacket. The photograph goes around the world. The Greens have arrived in power - and must now learn what power means: civil servants, budgets, compromises with the SPD and the Hanau nuclear plants that refuse to close themselves.",
"rotation_principle": "No professional politicians: every Green member of the Bundestag gives up the seat after two years to a successor from the list. It is grassroots democracy in its purest form - and it produces a parliamentary group that never learns the job, loses its best people just as they become effective and gives the other parties endless material for jokes.",
"green_unilateralism": "Swords into ploughshares: the Greens demand unilateral disarmament, the withdrawal of all nuclear weapons from German soil and an end to the blocs in East and West. Their Bundestag group tables the motions every month and loses them every month. In the peace movement, every defeat is a victory of conscience.",
"greens_merge": "1993: the West German Greens merge with the East German civil rights movement Alliance 90. The party of 1968 meets the people of 1989 - pastors, environmental activists and dissidents who brought down the SED. It is a union of very different biographies: one side fought the West's state, the other a dictatorship.",
"bielefeld_congress": "Bielefeld, May 1999. A paint bomb hits the Green foreign minister's ear. NATO is bombing Serbia, and the Bundeswehr is taking part in its first combat mission - with the approval of a red-green government. The party of peace must decide whether a Green may send the Luftwaffe to war. 'Never again war', say the delegates. 'Never again Auschwitz', answers the minister.",
"neckar_valley": "Research institutes, technology parks, venture capital from the Landesbank, engineers from Karlsruhe and Stuttgart: Baden-Württemberg is to be Germany's answer to California. The state invests in the future while others subsidise the past. Whether a Silicon Valley can be planned by a state government is the question nobody in Stuttgart likes to ask.",
"laender_partnership": "Baden-Württemberg adopts Saxony: civil servants, judges, tax inspectors and engineers on loan, a partnership treaty between the Länder and a Swabian accent in every ministry in Dresden. The West teaches the East how to run a federal state. Whether the East enjoys being taught is another matter.",
"jena_revival": "Lothar Späth goes east himself. He takes over the remains of Carl Zeiss Jena - the GDR's optical flagship, now facing the Treuhand's liquidators - and turns it into Jenoptik, a technology company that survives. Thousands of jobs are lost on the way; thousands are saved. Jena becomes proof that the East's industry need not die.",
"spaeth_chancellor": "September 1989: the CDU's reformers want Lothar Späth instead of Helmut Kohl. Geißler, Süssmuth and Späth himself are ready; at the Bremen party congress, in the decisive moment, they lose their nerve. Here they need not. The 'cleverle' from Stuttgart becomes chancellor just as the Wall begins to crumble.",
"donations_committee": "The Bundestag's committee of inquiry summons party treasurers, accountants, bankers and two former ministers. The Greens ask the questions; everyone else hopes for short memories. Hour by hour, on live television, the Republic learns how its parties were financed - and how much of it nobody remembers.",
"black_accounts": "Cash in suitcases, accounts in Liechtenstein and Switzerland, 'Jewish legacies' that turn out never to have existed. The former chancellor admits that he accepted millions in secret donations - and refuses to name the donors, because he gave them his word of honour. The party that ruled for sixteen years confronts the question of what it is built on.",
"transparency_act": "Every donation above ten thousand marks published within a month, cash donations banned, prison for false accounts, the party's entire assets disclosed every year. After the scandals the Bundestag writes the strictest party finance law in Europe. The party treasurers sigh. The voters may begin to trust again.",
"strauss_moscow_flight": "December 1987: the Bavarian premier pilots his own Cessna through a snowstorm to Moscow. The anti-communist of the 1950s, the man who wanted nuclear weapons for the Bundeswehr, sits down with Mikhail Gorbachev - and comes home impressed. If Strauß says the Soviet Union has changed, even his own voters begin to believe it.",
"wackersdorf": "A reprocessing plant for plutonium in the forests of the Upper Palatinate - Strauß's prestige project and the core of the German nuclear fuel cycle. It becomes the scene of the fiercest battles the anti-nuclear movement ever fought: tens of thousands in the woods, CS gas from helicopters, protests from neighbouring Austria. Bavaria never gives way, says the premier.",
"schmidt_alone": "The FDP has left the coalition; the chancellor stays. Helmut Schmidt governs with the SPD alone, from vote to vote, until the Bundestag either backs him or replaces him. It is the loneliest phase of his chancellorship - and in some ways the most impressive, as a man without a majority continues to govern by sheer authority.",
"schmidt_legacy": "Whatever happens in the Bundestag, the chancellor uses his last months for what he does best: the world economy, the security of Europe, a speech to the Bundestag that both sides will quote for years. History will judge Helmut Schmidt more kindly than his party does. He seems to know it.",
}
